### Shard 26 — Gear transactions, fulfilment and possession models > Edge-Case Coverage Matrix (2)
| Flow | Concurrent access | Invalid input / authority | Deletion, revocation or cascade |
|---|---|---|---|
| 26.01 Make/counter offer | Two counters on one offer serialize on the offer version; the loser refetches and re-counters rather than overwriting, so a party never accepts a price the other has already replaced. | An offer on a listing whose posture is gated returns the same typed refusal as one on a listing that does not exist, so the offer path cannot enumerate gated listings. | Withdrawing a listing closes open offers with the reason recorded; the offer history is retained, because it is the evidence of what was proposed at what price. |
| 26.02 Accept/decline offer | Acceptance is unique per offer: two acceptances collapse and exactly one order results, so a race cannot create two orders against one unit. | An acceptance against a superseded offer version returns a typed conflict and creates no order. An acceptance by a party without the buying-party authority is refused before any payment intent exists. | Declining is terminal for that offer and does not delete it; the unit returns to available and any competing offer stays live and independently acceptable. |
| 26.03 Maintain mixed-intent cart | Cart lines serialize per line, so two devices editing different lines do not contend; editing one line twice returns a typed conflict to the loser rather than merging quantities. | A seller can never read or write a buyer's cart at any authority level, and support never edits one — so the invalid-authority case here is a structural absence rather than a filtered denial. | A listing withdrawn while in a cart marks that line unavailable at the next read rather than silently vanishing, so the buyer sees what changed instead of a shrinking total. |
| 26.04 Resolve checkout eligibility | Eligibility is resolved atomically over the whole group against pinned line versions, so a line becoming ineligible mid-resolution invalidates that resolution rather than producing a partially eligible group. | An ineligible line names its blocking reason — posture, age or safeguarding eligibility, or destination — and the group does not proceed on the eligible subset unless the buyer explicitly splits it. | Removing a line re-resolves the whole group; eligibility is never carried forward from a resolution that included a line no longer present. |
| 26.05 Claim and authorize checkout | The unit claim is the decisive race and is serializable: exactly one checkout may hold a unit, so two buyers authorizing concurrently produce one order and one refusal, never an oversell. | Authorization is refused before any provider call when the acting party, buying party or eligibility check fails, so a failed authority check never leaves a pending payment intent. | An expired claim releases the unit and voids its authorization rather than leaving a held unit or a stranded intent; the released unit becomes claimable immediately. |
