### Shard 05 — Platform configuration, admin and quality > Contracts > Portability, Quality, and Retention
### Portability, Quality, and Retention

| Contract | Locked rule |
|---|---|
| Import | Explicit source/version/mapping/provenance/duplicate strategy; cannot import authority, ownership, consent, verification, money, rights or legal status as truth. |
| Export | Scope and field allowlist, actor/purpose, encryption, expiry/download limits, manifest/hashes; secrets/protected evidence separate or excluded. |
| Restore | Isolated target first; schema/media/reference/count/hash/RLS/render/accessibility validation before any promotion. Backup success without restore proof is failure. |
| Quality gate | Blocking rules: structural/schema/ref errors, required a11y metadata, broken/inaccessible block, privacy/legal/rights blockers; human review remains required. Readability/style warnings do not invent facts. |
| Retention | Record-class policy/rule-pack determines archive/delete/anonymize/hold/erasure; legal hold wins deletion but access is minimized/sealed; numeric periods counsel-gated. |

### Shard 04 — CMS navigation, media and delivery > Edge-Case Coverage Matrix (2)
| Flow | Concurrent access | Invalid input / authority | Deletion, revocation or cascade |
|---|---|---|---|
| DLV-01 Create/edit menu tree | Two editors drafting the same menu each write a new `MenuVersion` row, so drafts never overwrite one another and the `active` row is untouched by either. An edit to an existing draft carries that draft's expected `version`; the loser returns typed conflict with the current value, and no partial tree is written because the whole tree and its `tree_hash` commit together. | Depth > 3, > 200 items per tree, > 50 siblings, cycles or orphans reject the candidate before any row is written. `javascript:` and data-URL targets, non-HTTPS or open-redirect external hosts and preview/admin/private targets are not storable `target_kind` values, and a predicate outside the seven-value `visibility_predicate` vocabulary is refused — a rejected predicate never weakens the target's own independent authorization. | A target publication deleted or made private after the draft was written does not delete the draft; the draft stands and the eligibility check fails at DLV-02 preflight instead. Deleting a `Menu` cascades to its `MenuVersion` and `MenuItemVersion` rows, while `superseded` versions are retained because activation replaces pointers rather than deleting history. |
| DLV-02 Publish menu version | Two publishes racing for the same (`menu_id`, `locale`, `audience_class`) serialize on the expected currently-active manifest version: one activates and supersedes the prior row, the loser returns typed conflict and the prior active tree stands. Th…

…on. |
| DLV-03 Create/change slug | The normalized path reservation and the redirect source commit in one transaction against one `RouteManifestVersion`, so two writers claiming the same path serialize on the (`manifest_id`, `normalized_path`, `locale`) uniqueness constraint; the loser receives the exact safe conflict and no half-claimed slug survives. | A reserved or code-declared prefix, a locale-ambiguous normalization, an already-canonical path, a redirect cycle, a self-loop or a sixth hop rejects the manifest build as a whole; the last valid manifest keeps serving and nothing fails at request time. | Redirect history is permanent: a superseded slug keeps redirecting and a `RedirectRecord` is never deleted to free a path. Deleting the target publication leaves the redirect resolvable to an existence-safe not-found rather than silently retargeting it. |
| DLV-04 Configure discovery metadata | Unique on (`publication_id`, `locale`), so two authors editing one locale serialize on that uniqueness constraint — the entity …

…ld — and the loser returns typed conflict. Policy re-evaluation is idempotent — re-running it over an unchanged row yields the identical `policy_overrides` set. | Values outside the bounded title, description, card, breadcrumb, canonical, noindex and structured-data fields are refused. Unresolvable privacy, suppression, embargo, archive or legal state fails closed to `…

### Shard 05 — Platform configuration, admin and quality > Data Models > Typed Field and Cardinality Registry > `DataLifecycleRequest`
#### `DataLifecycleRequest`

| Field | Type | Constraint |
|---|---|---|
| `request_type` | `enum(archive, delete, anonymize, hold, release_hold, erasure)` | NOT NULL. Six distinct operations — none is an alias for another. |
| `requester_person_id` | `uuid` | FK → Shard 01 `PersonParty`. NOT NULL. |
| `subject_type` | `text` | NOT NULL. The owning entity kind of `subject_id`. **Open vocabulary, deliberately** — a retention, hold or erasure request reaches parties, records and content owned by other domains, so the admissible kinds are those domains' own entity names and belong to them rather than to this registry. A value is valid iff (`subject_type`, `subject_id`) resolves to a live row in the owning domain **and** `verification_ref` verifies that exact subject, which is why `verification_ref` is NOT NULL for `erasure` and `delete`; an unresolvable or unverified subject is refused **before any store acts**, because a store acting on a misidentified subject could not be undone. A subject that stops resolving mid-run never lets the request read as finished: the stores that cannot report leave it `partially_complete` with the gap named, never `complete`. |
| `subject_id` | `uuid` | NOT NULL. |
| `scope` | `jsonb` | NOT NULL. |
| `verification_ref` | `uuid` | NOT NULL for `erasure` and `delete`. |
| `store_manifest` | `jsonb` | NOT NULL, non-empty. Every store that must act. |
| `conflict_refs` | `uuid[]` | NOT NULL, possibly empty. An active legal `hold` conflicts with `delete`, `anonymize` and `erasure` and **wins**. |
| `decision_refs` | `uuid[]` | NOT NULL, possibly empty. |
| `state` | `enum(requested, blocked, running, complete, partially_complete, failed)` | NOT NULL. `partially_complete` is a real terminal state and never renders as complete. |
| `version` | `bigint` | NOT NULL, > 0. |

**Relationships.** N:1 to its subject and requester; 1:N to per-store results, each unique on (`request_id`, `store`, `action`) and carrying item count, state, evidence reference and error code. The request is only `complete` when every store in the manifest reports success — a store that never reported leaves the request `partially_complete` with the gap named.

